Engineer Input section contains only template placeholders, yet the technical approach cites Staff Engineer Input three times (CKS priority, observability as core value proposition, RHCL dependency). The cited directions were followed as part of the technical approach, but the underlying input is not in the strategy.\n2. **Schedule risk vs. Summit (May 2026)**: MaaS 3.4 GA stable-build timing is a PM-owned open question that does not change epic structure (AC on E003/E007) but is the strategy's self-declared critical blocker. Mitigations (RC by April 27, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are recorded as gate failure impacts.\n3. **Docs priority inversion (by rule)**: E011 is P0 because the docs-authoring priority exception derives priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Blocker` → P0), while its driving HLR is P2. This is rule-mandated, not a judgment call.\n4. **P1 logging bundled into P0 epic**: structured logging (P1) is bundled with metrics (P0) in E006 — same component, same team, same instrumentation surfaces; splitting would duplicate work on identical code paths. Noted so the P1 scope can be deferred independently if needed.\n5. **Pending human-review prerequisites**: Helm chart build pipeline (build onboarding) and license validation are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. Both are carried as ACs/notes on E004 rather than epics, since the models-as-a-service repo is already Konflux-onboarded; if the review requires a dedicated chart build pipeline effort, a follow-up epic may be needed.\n6. **xKS Platform installer integration not decomposed**: the Impacted Teams table assigns xKS Platform \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\", but no HLR or AC covers RHAII installer integration (Dev Preview validation deploys the MaaS chart directly). Flagged as potential follow-up scope, not invented as an epic.\n7. **Scope trap check**: the risk-table mitigation \"in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5min) for validated API key hashes\" is referenced by the PostgreSQL-unavailability failure-mode AC; it is carried as conditional scope on E005, not a separate epic.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nA classic Tier 1/Tier 2 split on the RHCL external dependency was evaluated and rejected as the primary structure: partial value without RHCL commitment is delivered via the fallback deployment path (standalone Authorino + Limitador), which is the same epic graph — the uncertainty is absorbed by E001's conditional outcomes rather than tiered epics. Platform phasing provides the effective tiering: **CKS validation (E007)** is the Summit-critical Tier 1 deliverable (priority platform per Staff Engineer Input); **AKS validation (E008)** follows serially; tracing (E010, P2) and docs (E011) complete the delivery.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":29700,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab07d7148cbf06fc","agent_id":"a5dd9ebccf03d402c","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"22f81083-bbc3-4f50-9f96-ffb8e8837551","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:54:25.443Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
