{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a514073e-2178-4d08-af2b-fddf47224945","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad09a28016eff420","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-oimz6d8o/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E005.md","content":"# External OIDC Authentication and Group Claim Mapping\n\n## Description\n\nReplaces MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity mechanism (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) with external OIDC validation, and makes the OIDC group claim path configurable so that tier/subscription mapping works across identity providers (Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims). The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*`) requires no changes — it already uses the maas-api callback validation path, which is OIDC-agnostic.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Configure the MaaS AuthPolicy generation to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, instead of KubernetesTokenReview (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md; deployment/base/maas-api/policies/auth-policy.yaml). The Tenant CRD already supports external OIDC configuration (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`), so basic OIDC integration is primarily configuration.\n- Add a `groupClaimPath` field to the Tenant CRD (default: `auth.identity.groups`) that is passed through to the MaaSSubscription controller's CEL predicate generation, so the claim path is configurable per deployment without code changes (architecture context: maas-controller/pkg/controller/maas/maassubscription_controller.go).\n- Ensure tier/subscription management maps rate limiting tiers to user groups sourced from the OIDC token's group claim rather than Kubernetes RBAC groups.\n- If the Tenant CRD is frozen in the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline (resolved by the baseline feasibility investigation), implement the documented fallback: document the required claim format and push IdP configuration to customers.\n- Confirm the API key lifecycle (`sk-oai-*` creation, validation, revocation) continues to function unchanged on xKS, including the Authorino HTTP callback to the maas-api key validation endpoint.\n- Resilience (from the strategy's failure-mode criteria and risk table): OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error when the IdP is unavailable while API key requests continue to succeed; if PostgreSQL unavailability testing shows excessive validation failures, implement the in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 min) for validated API key hashes per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured to match that format, when a user with group membership authenticates, then the MaaSSubscription controller correctly maps the user to the appropriate subscription tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), then inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n- Given a MaaS deployment, when the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n- Given a MaaS deployment with Limitador terminated, when a user makes an inference request, then the request is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and Envoy logs showing upstream connection failure.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity\n- [P0] API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS\n- [P0] Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping (supports Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims)\n- [P1] Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":56278,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab07d7148cbf06fc","agent_id":"a5dd9ebccf03d402c","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"bb75318a-56fb-4fda-b7ab-d55d218924bb","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:55:53.060Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
