{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a546cfd969903e7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 11\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8003 — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Result\n13\t\n14\tNot triaged. Strategy is M-sized, spans multiple components (models-as-a-service, Kuadrant/RHCL, Gateway API, observability) and three teams (MaaS, xKS Platform, RHCL/OpenShift), with multiple genuine unknowns that change downstream epic structure. Full decomposition follows.\n15\t\n16\t## Parsing Notes\n17\t\n18\t- **Staff Engineer Input section is empty** (template placeholder comments only). However, the AI-generated Technical Approach quotes Staff Engineer Input inline in several places (CKS as priority platform, observability as core value proposition, RHCL xKS support as strategic dependency). These inline quotes were treated as part of the strategy's technical approach; no separate Staff Engineer overrides existed to log. Flagged as a health warning (traceability gap).\n19\t- Component mappings to the canonical list in `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n20\t  - models-as-a-service (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing) → **Model as a Service** (direct match)\n21\t  - Kuadrant (Authorino, Limitador) / RHCL → **Inference Gateway** (no canonical Kuadrant/RHCL component exists; Inference Gateway is the closest parent-level component for gateway-level authentication and rate-limiting infrastructure on the MaaS gateway path)\n22\t  - Perses dashboards / CKS Prometheus / Loki integration → **Model and Agent Observability** (direct match)\n23\t  - User identity flow documentation → **Documentation** (docs work product)\n24\t- models-as-a-service exists in architecture context (`models-as-a-service.md`, `PLATFORM.md`) — no onboarding chain needed. Kuadrant has no architecture context file of its own but is an existing external RHCL component, not a new midstream component — handled as an investigation + deployment validation, not a repo-onboarding chain.\n25\t- The strategy's Prerequisites table lists ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding (Helm chart build pipeline) and Licence Validation as \"Pending human review\". The models-as-a-service repo already contains Konflux Dockerfiles per architecture context, so no `konflux-onboarding`/`license-validation` epic was generated; both are carried as acceptance criteria / health warnings.\n26\t\n27\t## Epic List\n28\t\n29\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n30\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n31\t| E001 | RHCL/Kuadrant xKS deployment path investigation | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n32\t| E002 | CKS observability infrastructure validation | Investigation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n33\t| E003 | MaaS 3.4 baseline feasibility investigation | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n34\t| E004 | MaaS Helm chart packaging for xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n35\t| E005 | External OIDC authentication and group claim mapping | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n36\t| E006 | MaaS observability instrumentation (metrics and structured logging) | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n37\t| E007 | MaaS deployment validation on CKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n38\t| E008 | MaaS deployment validation on AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n39\t| E009 | MaaS observability integration on xKS (dashboards, Prometheus, Loki) | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n40\t| E010 | Distributed tracing for the MaaS request path on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n41\t| E011 | MaaS on xKS user documentation (identity flow, known limitations) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n42\t| BRANCH-C-E012 | Contingency: direct Envoy ext_authz authentication and rate limiting | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n43\t\n44\tEpic count: 11 main-plan epics (12 including the branch contingency epic).\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t  E001 --> E005\n51\t  E001 --> E007\n52\t  E001 --> E008\n53\t  E002 --> E009\n54\t  E003 --> E005\n55\t  E003 --> E006\n56\t  E003 --> E007\n57\t  E004 --> E007\n58\t  E004 --> E008\n59\t  E005 --> E007\n60\t  E005 --> E008\n61\t  E006 --> E009\n62\t  E007 --> E008\n63\t  E007 --> E009\n64\t  E007 --> E010\n65\t  E004 --> E011\n66\t  E005 --> E011\n67\t  E006 --> E011\n68\t  E007 --> E011\n69\t  E008 --> E011\n70\t  E009 --> E011\n71\t  E010 --> E011\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\tBranch epic (not a main-plan node): `BRANCH-C-E012` depends on E001 (gated) and E004; selected only if the E001 investigation resolves to outcome C.\n75\t\n76\t## DAG Justification\n77\t\n78\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n79\t|------|------|-----------|\n80\t| E001 → E005 | 3 | Whether Kuadrant/Authorino is deployable on xKS determines whether the Authorino-based OIDC approach is viable at all; outcome C (Envoy ext_authz contingency) rewrites this epic's approach |\n81\t| E001 → E007 | 3 | The CKS validation epic deploys Kuadrant via the mechanism the investigation selects (RHCL Helm vs standalone vs contingency) — true gate, rewrite |\n82\t| E001 → E008 | 3 | Same gate applies to the AKS validation deployment |\n83\t| E002 → E009 | 3 | If CKS Prometheus/Loki are not accessible for MaaS, the observability integration approach changes (self-provisioned alternatives) — rewrite |\n84\t| E003 → E005 | 3 | Tenant CRD freeze status in MaaS 3.4 GA determines whether `groupClaimPath` is a code change or a documentation fallback — rewrite |\n85\t| E003 → E006 | 3 | maas-api instrumentation feasibility against the 3.4 GA code freeze determines in-tree vs post-3.4 patch vs deferral — rewrite |\n86\t| E003 → E007 | 3 | A usable 3.4 GA/RC baseline build is a precondition for CKS validation; if delayed, RC build or RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo is the remediation |\n87\t| E004 → E007 | 13 | The CKS validation deploys the Helm charts this epic produces — build-against artifact |\n88\t| E004 → E008 | 13 | Same for AKS validation |\n89\t| E005 → E007 | 12 | CKS validation exercises the OIDC authentication and group claim mapping this epic delivers |\n90\t| E005 → E008 | 12 | Same for AKS validation |\n91\t| E006 → E009 | 13 | Dashboards and Prometheus integration build against the metric names/contract this epic's instrumentation emits |\n92\t| E007 → E008 | strategy sequencing | Strategy and inline Staff Engineer Input mandate CKS-first validation; AKS validation consumes the CKS engineering delta and findings |\n93\t| E007 → E009 | 12/13 | Observability integration needs a running MaaS instance on CKS to scrape and query |\n94\t| E007 → E010 | 12 | Tracing validation needs the deployed request path (gateway → auth → payload-processing → backend) on CKS |\n95\t| E004..E010 → E011 | 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by all Implementation epics — docs describe what was built |\n96\t\n97\tNo edge between the two investigations and E004: Helm chart packaging of the MaaS components proceeds against the 3.4 RC build regardless of investigation outcomes (per the strategy's mitigation plan), and the chart itself is Kuadrant-mechanism-agnostic. No konflux-onboarding edges: the repo is already Konflux-built (architecture context confirms Dockerfile.konflux); \"build pipeline green\" is an AC on E004.\n98\t\n99\t## Investigation Outcomes and Conditional Branch\n100\t\n101\t**E001 (RHCL/Kuadrant xKS deployment path)** has three bounded outcomes per the strategy's priority-ordered deployment options:\n102\t\n103\t- **Outcome A — RHCL Helm charts committed**: Kuadrant deployed via official RHCL Helm charts.\n104\t- **Outcome B — fallback**: standalone Authorino + Limitador via their upstream Helm charts.\n105\t- **Outcome C — contingency**: Kuadrant not installable on xKS → direct Envoy ext_authz integration.\n106\t\n107\tOutcomes A and B are structurally identical for the main plan — both deploy Authorino + Limitador and differ only in chart provenance, which is an acceptance criterion on E007/E008 (\"Kuadrant deployed via the mechanism selected by the investigation\"), not a different epic graph. Outcome C changes the authentication architecture (no Authorino) and is modeled as the conditional branch epic **BRANCH-C-E012** (rewrite of the Authorino-based authentication approach delivered by E005), gated by E001. The strategy's escalation path (product leadership) and fallback demos are recorded as gate failure impacts, not epics.\n108\t\n109\t**E002** and **E003** have binary/qualitative outcomes whose fallbacks are pre-defined in the strategy (self-provisioned observability alternative; RC build / post-3.4 patch / RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) — captured as `gate_failure_impact` on the gated epics rather than branch files.\n110\t\n111\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n112\t\n113\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n114\t|-----|----------|---------|\n115\t| MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E007 |\n116\t| MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E008 |\n117\t| Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | E007, E008 (validation of Kuadrant Limitador, no code change per strategy) |\n118\t| External OIDC authentication validated | P0 | E005 (implementation), E007, E008 (validation) |\n119\t| API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E005 (unchanged path, OIDC-agnostic), E007, E008 (validation) |\n120\t| Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E005 |\n121\t| RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and validated | P0 | E001 |\n122\t| Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E006 (instrumentation), E009 (Prometheus integration/dashboards) |\n123\t| Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E005, E007 |\n124\t| Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E006 (maas-api structured logs), E009 (Loki integration incl. Authorino/Limitador logging config) |\n125\t| Known limitations documented | P1 | E007, E008 (validation deliverable), E011 (consolidation) |\n126\t| User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E011 |\n127\t| Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E010 |\n128\t\n129\tAll 13 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n130\t\n131\t## Health Warnings\n132\t\n133\t1. **Staff Engineer Input traceability gap**: the Staff Engineer Input section contains only template placeholders, yet the technical approach cites Staff Engineer Input three times (CKS priority, observability as core value proposition, RHCL dependency). The cited directions were followed as part of the technical approach, but the underlying input is not in the strategy.\n134\t2. **Schedule risk vs. Summit (May 2026)**: MaaS 3.4 GA stable-build timing is a PM-owned open question that does not change epic structure (AC on E003/E007) but is the strategy's self-declared critical blocker. Mitigations (RC by April 27, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are recorded as gate failure impacts.\n135\t3. **Docs priority inversion (by rule)**: E011 is P0 because the docs-authoring priority exception derives priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Blocker` → P0), while its driving HLR is P2. This is rule-mandated, not a judgment call.\n136\t4. **P1 logging bundled into P0 epic**: structured logging (P1) is bundled with metrics (P0) in E006 — same component, same team, same instrumentation surfaces; splitting would duplicate work on identical code paths. Noted so the P1 scope can be deferred independently if needed.\n137\t5. **Pending human-review prerequisites**: Helm chart build pipeline (build onboarding) and license validation are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. Both are carried as ACs/notes on E004 rather than epics, since the models-as-a-service repo is already Konflux-onboarded; if the review requires a dedicated chart build pipeline effort, a follow-up epic may be needed.\n138\t6. **xKS Platform installer integration not decomposed**: the Impacted Teams table assigns xKS Platform \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\", but no HLR or AC covers RHAII installer integration (Dev Preview validation deploys the MaaS chart directly). Flagged as potential follow-up scope, not invented as an epic.\n139\t7. **Scope trap check**: the risk-table mitigation \"in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5min) for validated API key hashes\" is referenced by the PostgreSQL-unavailability failure-mode AC; it is carried as conditional scope on E005, not a separate epic.\n140\t\n141\t## Tiered Delivery\n142\t\n143\tA classic Tier 1/Tier 2 split on the RHCL external dependency was evaluated and rejected as the primary structure: partial value without RHCL commitment is delivered via the fallback deployment path (standalone Authorino + Limitador), which is the same epic graph — the uncertainty is absorbed by E001's conditional outcomes rather than tiered epics. Platform phasing provides the effective tiering: **CKS validation (E007)** is the Summit-critical Tier 1 deliverable (priority platform per Staff Engineer Input); **AKS validation (E008)** follows serially; tracing (E010, P2) and docs (E011) complete the delivery.\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bff3f11e550c700","agent_id":"a83f285f4e7cc5ad9","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"3bd3da2f-9aa4-430a-9221-c76498b03095","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:01:45.661Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a546cfd969903e7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
