{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a6a1501a4f0ddc3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E005\n3\ttitle: External OIDC authentication and group claim mapping\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If Kuadrant/Authorino cannot be deployed on xKS, the Authorino-based\n16\t    OIDC approach is replaced by the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency. If the Tenant\n17\t    CRD is frozen in the 3.4 baseline, the groupClaimPath code change falls back to\n18\t    documenting the required OIDC claim format and pushing IdP configuration to customers.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# External OIDC Authentication and Group Claim Mapping\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tReplaces MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity mechanism (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) with external OIDC validation, and makes the OIDC group claim path configurable so that tier/subscription mapping works across identity providers (Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims). The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*`) requires no changes — it already uses the maas-api callback validation path, which is OIDC-agnostic.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Configure the MaaS AuthPolicy generation to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, instead of KubernetesTokenReview (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md; deployment/base/maas-api/policies/auth-policy.yaml). The Tenant CRD already supports external OIDC configuration (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`), so basic OIDC integration is primarily configuration.\n45\t- Add a `groupClaimPath` field to the Tenant CRD (default: `auth.identity.groups`) that is passed through to the MaaSSubscription controller's CEL predicate generation, so the claim path is configurable per deployment without code changes (architecture context: maas-controller/pkg/controller/maas/maassubscription_controller.go).\n46\t- Ensure tier/subscription management maps rate limiting tiers to user groups sourced from the OIDC token's group claim rather than Kubernetes RBAC groups.\n47\t- If the Tenant CRD is frozen in the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline (resolved by the baseline feasibility investigation), implement the documented fallback: document the required claim format and push IdP configuration to customers.\n48\t- Confirm the API key lifecycle (`sk-oai-*` creation, validation, revocation) continues to function unchanged on xKS, including the Authorino HTTP callback to the maas-api key validation endpoint.\n49\t- Resilience (from the strategy's failure-mode criteria and risk table): OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error when the IdP is unavailable while API key requests continue to succeed; if PostgreSQL unavailability testing shows excessive validation failures, implement the in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 min) for validated API key hashes per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n54\t- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured to match that format, when a user with group membership authenticates, then the MaaSSubscription controller correctly maps the user to the appropriate subscription tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n55\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), then inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n56\t- Given a MaaS deployment, when the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n57\t- Given a MaaS deployment with Limitador terminated, when a user makes an inference request, then the request is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and Envoy logs showing upstream connection failure.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity\n62\t- [P0] API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS\n63\t- [P0] Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping (supports Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims)\n64\t- [P1] Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bff3f11e550c700","agent_id":"a83f285f4e7cc5ad9","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"f2403cae-5aa4-484c-838b-a5be6246f4b1","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:02:00.201Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a6a1501a4f0ddc3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
