c to P2 tracing (separate epic E010) but not to P1 logging, which is inconsistent. E006 and E009 should each carry the P1 logging scope in a separately plannable epic (or the split done along the metrics/logging axis). Per the scoring rule, a major issue forces 0 for this criterion.\n\nNote: the P1 scope bundled into E005 (tier/subscription with OIDC group claims) and E007 (tier/subscription validation, known-limitations deliverable) is incidental to the P0 work it rides on (the groupClaimPath mechanism and the validation exercise itself) and is not flagged.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nThe DAG is acyclic. Every edge in the summary diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa (verified edge-by-edge for all 21 main-plan edges); arrow direction follows the dependency→dependent convention. No main-plan epic references a branch epic in `dependencies`. BRANCH-C-E012 correctly sets both `branch: C` and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001`, with the gate target in its direct dependencies. There are no konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints do not apply; the repo is already Konflux-built (confirmed in architecture context: Dockerfile.konflux for maas-api and maas-controller) and the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC is present on E004. The E007→E008 serialization is justified by the strategy's explicit CKS-first mandate. E001→E008 is transitive via E007 but harmless. Critical path (E001→E005→E007→E008→E011, length 5) sits at the top of the M band, driven by the rule-mandated docs edges and strategy-mandated CKS→AKS sequencing — acceptable, noted as a minor observation along with the redundant transitive edges (E004→E008, E005→E008 already implied via E007).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples produce separate epics (E004/E005/E006 MaaS-implementation vs E002/E009 xKS Platform observability vs E001 RHCL investigation); no epic is oversized; no work-product type mixing (docs are isolated in E011); no scope duplication between siblings (E005 implements / E007-E008 validate; E006 emits metrics / E009 integrates them — producer/consumer pairs, not duplicate builds).\n\n**Boundary edge case (costs the point):** E009 (team: xKS Platform) includes \"the structured logging configuration for Authorino (authentication decisions) and Limitador (rate limit enforcement)\" — work the strategy's Affected Components table assigns to RHCL / OpenShift (external), which the strategy's Impacted Teams row describes as critical RHCL involvement. The epic acknowledges RHCL \"configuration guidance\" but attributes the work to a single team where two contribute. Splitting the Authorino/Limitador logging configuration out (or explicitly co-owning it) would fix this.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n\nAll three Investigations genuinely resolve structural uncertainty (E001 selects the deployment mechanism and gates the outcome-C branch; E002 and E003 change downstream approaches), and all Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Branch/gate metadata on E006, E008, E009, E010, E011, and BRANCH-C-E012 is correct.\n\n**Major — ungated Investigation dependency (E005):** E005 lists Investigation E003 in `dependencies` but `gated_by` is set only to E001. E003's outcome (Tenant CRD freeze in the 3.4 baseline) changes E005's scope — E005's own `gate_failure_impact` text says the groupClaimPath work \"falls back to documenting the required OIDC claim format\" if the CRD is frozen, and the DAG justification labels E003→E005 a rule-3 \"rewrite\" edge. By definition a true gate must be reflected in `gated_by`; automated consumers will not detect the E003 gate.\n\n**Major — ungated Investigation dependency (E007):** same pattern: E007 depends on E003 (baseline build availability) with `gated_by` only E001, yet its `gate_failure_impact` describes the RC-build/RHOAI-3.3-fallback remediation for exactly the E003 outcome — the outcome changes E007's baseline and scope.\n\nPer the scoring rule, a major issue forces 0 for this criterion.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nSignal fields are type-correct across the board (Investigations carry `investigation_signals`, Implementations including docs-authoring E011 carry `ai_signals`), all 13 ai-signals files exist, and rationales match the frontmatter values. Spot-checks against strategy content and architecture context hold up: E004's `existing_foundation: 1` (extends existing manifests; Dockerfile.konflux confirmed), E005's `pattern_precedent: 1` (optional OIDC JWT already in the AuthPolicy per models-as-a-service.md), E006's `pattern_precedent: 1` (/metrics route on port 9090 confirmed), E009's `architecture_claims: 0` (observability stack not covered by the component's architecture context), E010's `change_specificity: 0` (tracing backend unspecified in strategy).\n\n**Arguable value (costs the point):** E003's `source_accessibility: 1`. One of its three gating questions — the stable MaaS 3.4 build date — is a pending human (PM) decision that does not yet exist in readable form, which the rationale itself concedes (\"only build timing needs PM input\"). With a third of the investigation's answer resting on a human decision rather than readable sources, 0 would be the more defensible value; this is borderline rather than a contradiction.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable Given/When/Then acceptance criteria with explicit measures, derived from the strategy's own happy-path and failure-mode criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present: backwards-compatibility/no-regression AC for the OLM→Helm packaging change (E004), technical-review-before-publication AC for docs-authoring (E011), and build-pipeline-green AC for the Helm chart (E004). Failure-mode criteria (IdP unavailability, Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability) are carried into E005/E007. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — cross-epic references use full titles (e.g., \"the baseline feasibility investigation\"), so nothing is lost when Jira keys are assigned.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nFull scope coverage: both deployment validations (CKS, AKS), OIDC authentication, group claim mapping, API key flow, rate limiting, tier/subscription management, all three observability tiers (metrics/logging/tracing), known-limitations and identity-flow documentation, the scope-reduction exclusions respected (no operator work on xKS, no Dashboard UI), and all strategy failure modes carried. Strategy-level context is carried forward well: risks (LRU cache → conditional scope on E005; async emission/p99 threshold → E006; RHCL escalation → E001), assumptions map to the three investigations, and the shared-vs-dedicated PostgreSQL open question is resolved as an E004 configuration AC. The conditional branch covers all three bounded outcomes of the E001 investigation (A/B structurally identical in the main plan with the mechanism as an AC; C as BRANCH-C-E012). Docs-authoring epic present (E011). Two minor observations, neither a scope gap: the \"OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages\" open question is not explicitly carried into any epic's scope, and the xKS Platform installer integration mentioned in Impacted Teams is transparently flagged as potential follow-up rather than silently dropped.\n\n## Issue Summary\n\nEight issues: 3 major (criterion 4 forced to 0 twice, criterion 1 once), 1 boundary edge case, 4 minor. The major issues are all correctable without restructuring the epic graph: set `gated_by` (or split gates) for the E003 dependencies on E005 and E007, and split the P1 logging scope out of E006/E009 into independently plannable epics.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":59061,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bff3f11e550c700","agent_id":"a83f285f4e7cc5ad9","