{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-2e9ad50c-26c4-45d6-bb88-2a7887642d6c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-92895064f4c82506","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-oimz6d8o/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E005.md","old_string":"- Resilience (from the strategy's failure-mode criteria and risk table): OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error when the IdP is unavailable while API key requests continue to succeed; if PostgreSQL unavailability testing shows excessive validation failures, implement the in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 min) for validated API key hashes per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated API key.\n- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured to match that format, when a user with group membership authenticates, then the MaaSSubscription controller correctly maps the user to the appropriate subscription tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), then inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n- Given a MaaS deployment, when the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n- Given a MaaS deployment with Limitador terminated, when a user makes an inference request, then the request is denied with HTTP 503, measured by response status and Envoy logs showing upstream connection failure.","new_string":"- Resilience (from the strategy's failure-mode criteria and risk table): OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error when the IdP is unavailable while API key requests continue to succeed; if PostgreSQL unavailability testing shows excessive validation failures, implement the in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 min) for validated API key hashes per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n- Resolve and document the strategy's open question on the OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages (owner: Security / MaaS Engineering): determine whether and for how long Authorino caches OIDC validation results (e.g., JWKS caching) and the resulting behavior of OIDC-authenticated requests during IdP downtime, consistent with the failure-mode behavior that API key requests are unaffected.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the MaaS AuthPolicy generation with an external OIDC identity source configured (issuer URL, client ID, audience, JWKS endpoint), when the generated AuthPolicy is inspected, then it configures Authorino's `oidc` identity source instead of KubernetesTokenReview, measured by diffing the rendered AuthPolicy against the expected OIDC identity-source configuration.\n- Given the switch to the OIDC identity source, when the API key callback configuration is inspected, then the `sk-oai-*` validation path (Authorino HTTP callback to the maas-api key validation endpoint) is unchanged and OIDC-agnostic, measured by configuration inspection.\n- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured to match that format, when a user with group membership authenticates, then the MaaSSubscription controller correctly maps the user to the appropriate subscription tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), then inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n- Given a MaaS deployment, when the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n- The OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages is determined and documented (whether Authorino caches JWKS/validation results and for how long OIDC-authenticated requests can be served during IdP downtime), measured by a documented decision reflected in the AuthPolicy/OIDC configuration."}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62909,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9dff2f9036b28837","agent_id":"a9d6226c814588f52","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"0cf2536e-a503-4bbe-944d-46e8992fa6c5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:20:27.152Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
