o main-plan epic lists the branch epic in `dependencies`. Critical path (5 nodes, e.g. E001→E005→E007→E008→E011) is at the upper bound of the M heuristic but the terminal node is the rule-mandated docs edge, so the implementation critical path is 4 — acceptable.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples map cleanly to separate epics: MaaS-owned implementation work (E004–E008, E010, E013) vs xKS Platform integration work (E009) vs RHCL/OpenShift investigation (E001) vs docs (E011). No scope duplication between siblings — E006/E013 (metrics vs logging instrumentation), E009/E014 (Prometheus/dashboards vs Loki), and E007/E008 (CKS vs AKS) are disjoint along clean axes. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring; E011 is pure docs-authoring. Epic sizes are within a sprint each.\n\nOne minor boundary note: E014 is co-owned by \"xKS Platform / RHCL / OpenShift\" and bundles the RHCL-owned Authorino/Limitador logging configuration (which the strategy's Affected Components table assigns to the Kuadrant components under RHCL/OpenShift) with the xKS-Platform-owned Loki integration under the \"Model and Agent Observability\" component. This qualifies as the same logical change (one logging-integration deliverable, one AC set) and is explicitly documented, but it re-couples exactly the external dependency the E013/E014 split was meant to make independently plannable — if RHCL is slow, the xKS-owned Loki integration is blocked with it. A split along the team line would restore independent plannability.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll three Investigations are genuine: E001's outcome selects between deployment mechanisms and triggers the conditional branch (rewrite of E005/E007/E008 approach); E002's outcome changes the E009/E014 integration approach (self-provisioned fallback); E003's outcome changes the E005/E006/E013 approach (in-tree vs post-3.4 patch vs deferral) and E007's baseline. None is merely informational. All Implementation epics p