{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9799cea89cae4ff8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n9\t  description: 'E005 replaces the KubernetesTokenReview identity mechanism with external\n10\t    OIDC but lacks the rule-mandated rollback/feature-flag AC: nothing verifies that\n11\t    deployments without OIDC configured retain the existing identity behavior and\n12\t    that RHOAI MaaS GA authentication is unchanged (strategy NFR: xKS changes must\n13\t    be additive or configuration-driven). E004 carries the analogous AC for the OLM-to-Helm\n14\t    replacement; E005 must carry one for the identity-source replacement.'\n15\t- severity: minor\n16\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n17\t  description: E010 scores pattern_precedence 0 and existing_foundation 0 describing\n18\t    the work as greenfield with no existing OTel instrumentation, but architecture\n19\t    context documents existing OpenTelemetry tracing in maas-api (internal/tracing/provider.go,\n20\t    OTLP/gRPC export, go.opentelemetry.io deps). The E010 path excludes maas-api so\n21\t    the values are arguable rather than wrong, but existing_foundation is a borderline\n22\t    call against a codebase that already ships an OTel provider.\n23\t- severity: minor\n24\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n25\t  description: E005 pattern_precedent rationale (and the epic description and scope)\n26\t    states the architecture context shows the Tenant CRD already carries OIDC fields\n27\t    (oidcIssuerURL, oidcClientID, oidcAudience); no such fields appear in the architecture\n28\t    context. Only the AuthPolicy-level optional OIDC JWT validation is confirmed.\n29\t    Soften to a strategy claim pending E003 verification, or add verifying the Tenant\n30\t    CRD OIDC fields to E003 scope.\n31\t- severity: minor\n32\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n33\t  description: E014 is co-owned by xKS Platform / RHCL / OpenShift and bundles the\n34\t    RHCL-assigned Authorino/Limitador logging configuration (Kuadrant components)\n35\t    with the xKS-Platform-owned Loki integration under the Model and Agent Observability\n36\t    component. Same-logical-change bundling and documented, but it re-couples the\n37\t    external RHCL dependency the E013/E014 split was meant to make independently plannable;\n38\t    consider splitting along the team line.\n39\terror: null\n40\t---\n41\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8003 — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n42\t\n43\t## Review Summary\n44\t\n45\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n46\tRecommendation: revise\n47\t\n48\tOne major issue (missing rule-mandated acceptance criterion on E005) forces Criterion 6 to 0; under the auto-fail rule the decomposition must be revised despite the otherwise strong total.\n49\t\n50\t## Criterion Details\n51\t\n52\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n53\t\n54\tAll 13 HLRs from the strategy are covered by the epic set and the traceability matrix in the decomposition summary is complete and consistent with the per-epic \"HLR Traceability\" sections: P0 CKS deployment (E007), P0 AKS deployment (E008), P0 token rate limiting (E007/E008, plus BRANCH-C-E012 contingency), P0 external OIDC (E005/E007/E008), P0 API key flow (E005/E007/E008), P0 group claim path (E005), P0 RHCL support status (E001), P0 observability metrics (E006/E009), P1 tier/subscription management (E005/E007), P1 observability logging (E013/E014), P1 known limitations (E007/E008/E011), P2 identity flow documentation (E011), P2 tracing (E010).\n55\t\n56\tPriority inheritance is correct throughout: all investigations gating P0 work (E001, E002, E003) are P0; E010 (P2) and E013/E014 (P1) sit behind P0 prerequisites, never the reverse. E011's P0 priority is the rule-mandated docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Blocker → P0), correctly documented in Health Warning #3. Priority collapse was handled well: the P1 structured-logging scope was split out of the P0 metrics epics into E013/E014 so it is independently deferrable, and the P1 tier-mapping HLR inside E005 is the same code path (CEL predicate generation driven by `groupClaimPath`), not a distinct deferrable feature. Minor observation, not scored: E002's own HLR Traceability claims the P0 metrics and P1 logging HLRs as \"prerequisite validation\" while the summary matrix attributes those HLRs only to E006/E009 and E013/E014 — a labeling inconsistency, not a coverage gap.\n57\t\n58\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n59\t\n60\tThe diagram and frontmatter are fully consistent: every edge in the summary DAG has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic and vice versa (E001–E003, E004 have empty deps; E005 [E001,E003]; E006 [E003]; E007 [E001,E003,E004,E005]; E008 [E001,E007]; E009 [E002,E006,E007]; E010 [E007]; E011 [E004..E010,E013,E014]; E013 [E003]; E014 [E002,E007,E013]). Arrows run dependency → dependent, matching the convention. No cycles. All gating edges are justified against the construction rules: investigation → gated implementation (rule 3), artifact-consumer edges E004→E007, E006→E009, E013→E014 (rule 13), E007→E008 (strategy-mandated CKS-first sequencing), and the rule-11 docs edges into E011.\n61\t\n62\tParallelism is respected: E004 (packaging) correctly has no edge to the investigations (justified — the chart is mechanism-agnostic and the strategy directs packaging against the RC build in parallel); E005/E006/E013 run in parallel off E003; E009 and E014 are parallel P1-deferrable consumers. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 have no applicable pairs — and the omission is properly justified (architecture context confirms `Dockerfile.konflux` for maas-api and maas-controller, so no onboarding chain is needed; the \"build pipeline green\" AC is present on E004). The removed transitive edges (E004→E008, E005→E008) are legitimate transitively-redundant edges; keeping E001→E008 is required because E008 is gated_by E001.\n63\t\n64\tConditional branch handling is correct: BRANCH-C-E012 sets both `branch: C` and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001`, includes the gating investigation in its dependencies, and no main-plan epic lists the branch epic in `dependencies`. Critical path (5 nodes, e.g. E001→E005→E007→E008→E011) is at the upper bound of the M heuristic but the terminal node is the rule-mandated docs edge, so the implementation critical path is 4 — acceptable.\n65\t\n66\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n67\t\n68\tComponent/team tuples map cleanly to separate epics: MaaS-owned implementation work (E004–E008, E010, E013) vs xKS Platform integration work (E009) vs RHCL/OpenShift investigation (E001) vs docs (E011). No scope duplication between siblings — E006/E013 (metrics vs logging instrumentation), E009/E014 (Prometheus/dashboards vs Loki), and E007/E008 (CKS vs AKS) are disjoint along clean axes. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring; E011 is pure docs-authoring. Epic sizes are within a sprint each.\n69\t\n70\tOne minor boundary note: E014 is co-owned by \"xKS Platform / RHCL / OpenShift\" and bundles the RHCL-owned Authorino/Limitador logging configuration (which the strategy's Affected Components table assigns to the Kuadrant components under RHCL/OpenShift) with the xKS-Platform-owned Loki integration under the \"Model and Agent Observability\" component. This qualifies as the same logical change (one logging-integration deliverable, one AC set) and is explicitly documented, but it re-couples exactly the external dependency the E013/E014 split was meant to make independently plannable — if RHCL is slow, the xKS-owned Loki integration is blocked with it. A split along the team line would restore independent plannability.\n71\t\n72\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n73\t\n74\tAll three Investigations are genuine: E001's outcome selects between deployment mechanisms and triggers the conditional branch (rewrite of E005/E007/E008 approach); E002's outcome changes the E009/E014 integration approach (self-provisioned fallback); E003's outcome changes the E005/E006/E013 approach (in-tree vs post-3.4 patch vs deferral) and E007's baseline. None is merely informational. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (Helm charts, code/instrumentation, validated deployments, dashboards, docs, configuration). Gating metadata is complete and correct: every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set (E005, E006, E007, E008, E009, E013, E014, BRANCH-C-E012); every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`; every `gate_failure_impact` has both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated with strategy-sourced fallbacks. No scheduling dependency is mislabeled as a gate.\n75\t\n76\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n77\t\n78\tSignal fields are type-appropriate everywhere: the three Investigations carry `investigation_signals` (null `ai_signals`) and all Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals rationale tables in every file. Notably correct: E001 and E002 mark `source_accessibility: 0` for answers that are pending human/empirical validation rather than readable — exactly the rubric's failure case avoided. Two arguable values, however:\n79\t\n80\t- E010 (tracing) scores `pattern_precedent: 0` (\"No existing OpenTelemetry instrumentation in the MaaS request path\") and `existing_foundation: 0` (\"Greenfield tracing instrumentation across multiple components\"), but the architecture context documents existing OpenTelemetry tracing in maas-api (`maas-api/internal/tracing/provider.go`, OTLP/gRPC export to an OTel Collector, `go.opentelemetry.io/*` v1.44.0 dependencies). E010's enumerated path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) excludes maas-api, so the values are defensible, but \"no existing OTel instrumentation\" overstates the greenfield nature of a codebase that already ships an OTel provider — `existing_foundation` is a borderline call.\n81\t- E005's `pattern_precedent: 1` rationale asserts \"Architecture context shows the Tenant CRD already carries OIDC fields\". The architecture context contains no `oidcIssuerURL`/`oidcClientID`/`oidcAudience` Tenant CRD fields; what it does confirm is optional OIDC JWT validation at the AuthPolicy level. The value 1 remains supportable on the confirmed half, but the rationale (and the epic description/scope, which repeat the Tenant-CRD claim as fact) cites architecture evidence that is not there. Consider softening to a strategy claim pending E003 verification.\n82\t\n83\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (0/2)\n84\t\n85\tACs across the epic set are specific, testable, and traceable to the strategy's happy-path and failure-mode criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present for docs-authoring (E011: \"technically reviewed against the actual implementation ... before publication\") and the build chain (E004: \"Build pipeline for the Helm chart artifacts is green\"). E004, which replaces OLM-based installation, also correctly carries the backwards-compatibility AC (\"RHOAI MaaS GA deployments are unchanged ... no regression to the OLM-based path\").\n86\t\n87\tHowever, E005 — which by its own description \"Replaces MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity mechanism (KubernetesTokenReview ...) with external OIDC validation\" — lacks the rule-mandated rollback/feature-flag AC. The strategy's NFR explicitly requires \"No changes to RHOAI MaaS GA — xKS changes must be additive or configuration-driven\", and the architecture context confirms the switch is configuration-driven (\"optional OIDC JWT validation when an OIDC provider is configured\"), so the AC is trivially writable (e.g., tenants/deployments without OIDC configured retain the existing identity behavior; RHOAI MaaS GA authentication is unchanged). E005's AC set verifies the new OIDC behavior and the unchanged API-key callback path, but nothing protects the existing TokenReview/RHOAI behavior from regression by the AuthPolicy-generation and CRD code changes. This is a major issue: the rule applies to every replacing epic, not only the most obvious one (E004 got the AC; E005 did not). Per the scoring rules, a major issue forces this criterion to 0.\n88\t\n89\tNo other rule-mandated AC gaps found (BRANCH-C-E012 is a contingency path, not a replacement of an existing production capability). No draft-ID references to sibling epics appear in any epic body.\n90\t\n91\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n92\t\n93\tThe epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: user management strategy (E005), Helm packaging (E004), CKS and AKS validation (E007/E008, satisfying the \"AKS AND CKS\" RFE criterion), RHCL strategic dependency with all three bounded outcomes (E001 + BRANCH-C-E012 for outcome C, A/B absorbed as an AC on the validation epics), observability metrics/logging/tracing (E006/E009, E013/E014, E010), documentation (E011). All strategy acceptance criteria — happy path and failure modes (OIDC outage, Limitador termination, cert rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability) — map to epic ACs. Strategy-level context is carried forward rather than dropped: risk mitigations appear as conditional scope (LRU cache on E005, p99 5 ms on E006/E013, escalation path in E001's gate), and the open questions are distributed to the right epics (OIDC caching → E005, shared/dedicated PostgreSQL → E004 AC, stable build date → E003, RHCL charts → E001, CKS Prometheus/Loki → E002). The xKS-Platform installer integration mentioned only in the Impacted Teams table is transparently flagged as a potential follow-up (Health Warning #6) rather than silently dropped or invented — correct handling of out-of-HLR scope. Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not \"Yes\") and a docs-authoring epic exists regardless. Minor observation, not scored: the strategy is internally inconsistent on tracing priority (NFR says P1, HLR list and Technical Approach say P2); the decomposition followed the HLR list, which is the defensible choice.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a96c07aedf57c588","agent_id":"a43da7aa6bd0b6b38","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"5ec8bc2a-3c2a-42f7-a22a-46a0995a0ba7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:37:00.406Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9799cea89cae4ff8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Second revise RHAISTRAT-8003"}
