uthentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E005 (unchanged path, OIDC-agnostic), E007, E008 (validation) |\n136\t| Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | E005 |\n137\t| RHCL official xKS support status confirmed and validated | P0 | E001 |\n138\t| Observability metrics functional (token usage, rate limiting, API key lifecycle) | P0 | E006 (instrumentation), E009 (Prometheus integration/dashboards) |\n139\t| Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | E005, E007 |\n140\t| Observability logging functional (auth events, rate limit enforcement, subscription selection) | P1 | E013 (maas-api structured logs), E014 (Loki integration), E015 (Authorino/Limitador logging configuration, RHCL/OpenShift-owned) |\n141\t| Known limitations documented | P1 | E007, E008 (validation deliverable), E011 (consolidation) |\n142\t| User identity flow documented for xKS deployments | P2 | E011 |\n143\t| Distributed tracing functional (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend) | P2 | E010 |\n144\t\n145\tAll 13 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n146\t\n147\t## Health Warnings\n148\t\n149\t1. **Staff Engineer Input traceability gap**: the Staff Engineer Input section contains only template placeholders, yet the technical approach cites Staff Engineer Input three times (CKS priority, observability as core value proposition, RHCL dependency). The cited directions were followed as part of the technical approach, but the underlying input is not in the strategy.\n150\t2. **Schedule risk vs. Summit (May 2026)**: MaaS 3.4 GA stable-build timing is a PM-owned open question that does not change epic structure (AC on E003/E007) but is the strategy's self-declared critical blocker. Mitigations (RC by April 27, parallel CKS validation, RHOAI 3.3 fallback demo) are recorded as gate failure impacts.\n151\t3. **Docs priority inversion (by rule)**: E011 is P0 because the docs-authoring priority exception derives priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Blocker` → P0), while its driving HLR is P2. This is rule-mandated, not a judgment call.\n152\t4. **P1 logging split into separately plannable epics (resolved during revision)**: structured logging (P1) was originally bundled with metrics (P0) in E006/E009. Per review feedback it is now split into E013 (maas-api structured-logging instrumentation), E014 (Loki integration, xKS Platform-owned), and E015 (Authorino/Limitador logging configuration, RHCL/OpenShift-owned per the strategy's Affected Components table), so the P1 logging scope can be deferred independently per the strategy's risk table without touching the P0 observability epics, and the xKS-owned Loki integration is independently plannable from the external RHCL dependency.\n153\t5. **Pending human-review prerequisites**: Helm chart build pipeline (build onboarding) and license validation are \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. Both are carried as ACs/notes on E004 rather than epics, since the models-as-a-service repo is already Konflux-onboarded; if the review requires a dedicated chart build pipeline effort, a follow-up epic may be needed.\n154\t6. **xKS Platform installer integration not decomposed**: the Impacted Teams table assigns xKS Platform \"Helm chart integration with RHAII xKS deployment strategy\", but no HLR or AC covers RHAII installer integration (Dev Preview validation deploys the MaaS chart directly). Flagged as potential follow-up scope, not invented as an epic.\n155\t7. **Scope trap check**: the risk-table mitigation \"in-memory LRU cache (TTL 5min) for validated API key hashes\" is referenced by the PostgreSQL-unavailability failure-mode AC; it is carried as conditional scope on E005, not a separate epic.\n156\t\n157\t## Tiered Delivery\n158\t\n159\tA classic Tier 1/Tier 2 split on the RHCL external dependency was evaluated and rejected as the primary structure: partial value without RHCL commitment is delivered via the fallback deployment path (standalone Authorino + Limitador), which is the same epic graph — the uncertainty is absorbed by E001's conditional outcomes rather than tiered epics. Platform phasing provides the effective tiering: **CKS validation (E007)** is the Summit-critical Tier 1 deliverable (priority platform per Staff Engineer Input); **AKS validation (E008)** follows serially; the P1 logging scope (E013/E014/E015) is independently deferrable per the strategy's risk table; tracing (E010, P2) and docs (E011) complete the delivery.\n160\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-817539f93779a1be","agent_id":"a91a5677bf1f7de4b","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"4a97d6a1-45a0-41af-9071-fc1d593a10ff","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:53:05.842Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cf6830a1d3fe18f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Third revise RHAISTRAT-8003"}
