{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b20549ac313a6d43","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E005\n3\ttitle: External OIDC authentication and group claim mapping\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n5\tcomponent: Model as a Service\n6\tteam: MaaS\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001,RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If Kuadrant/Authorino cannot be deployed on xKS, the Authorino-based\n16\t    OIDC approach is replaced by the direct Envoy ext_authz contingency. If the Tenant\n17\t    CRD is frozen in the 3.4 baseline, the groupClaimPath code change falls back to\n18\t    documenting the required OIDC claim format and pushing IdP configuration to customers.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 5\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# External OIDC Authentication and Group Claim Mapping\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tReplaces MaaS's OpenShift-specific user identity mechanism (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) with external OIDC validation, and makes the OIDC group claim path configurable so that tier/subscription mapping works across identity providers (Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims). The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*`) requires no changes — it already uses the maas-api callback validation path, which is OIDC-agnostic.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Configure the MaaS AuthPolicy generation to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source with the customer's OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint, instead of KubernetesTokenReview (architecture context: models-as-a-service.md; deployment/base/maas-api/policies/auth-policy.yaml — which already supports optional OIDC JWT validation). The strategy claims the Tenant CRD supports external OIDC configuration (`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`); this is a strategy claim, not confirmed by architecture context, and is verified against the 3.4 baseline by the baseline feasibility investigation — if confirmed, basic OIDC integration is primarily configuration.\n45\t- Add a `groupClaimPath` field to the Tenant CRD (default: `auth.identity.groups`) that is passed through to the MaaSSubscription controller's CEL predicate generation, so the claim path is configurable per deployment without code changes (architecture context: maas-controller/pkg/controller/maas/maassubscription_controller.go).\n46\t- Ensure tier/subscription management maps rate limiting tiers to user groups sourced from the OIDC token's group claim rather than Kubernetes RBAC groups.\n47\t- If the Tenant CRD is frozen in the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline (resolved by the baseline feasibility investigation), implement the documented fallback: document the required claim format and push IdP configuration to customers.\n48\t- Confirm the API key lifecycle (`sk-oai-*` creation, validation, revocation) continues to function unchanged on xKS, including the Authorino HTTP callback to the maas-api key validation endpoint.\n49\t- Resilience (from the strategy's failure-mode criteria and risk table): OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error when the IdP is unavailable while API key requests continue to succeed; if PostgreSQL unavailability testing shows excessive validation failures, implement the in-memory LRU cache (TTL: 5 min) for validated API key hashes per the strategy's risk mitigation.\n50\t- Resolve and document the strategy's open question on the OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages (owner: Security / MaaS Engineering): determine whether and for how long Authorino caches OIDC validation results (e.g., JWKS caching) and the resulting behavior of OIDC-authenticated requests during IdP downtime, consistent with the failure-mode behavior that API key requests are unaffected.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Given the MaaS AuthPolicy generation with an external OIDC identity source configured (issuer URL, client ID, audience, JWKS endpoint), when the generated AuthPolicy is inspected, then it configures Authorino's `oidc` identity source instead of KubernetesTokenReview, measured by diffing the rendered AuthPolicy against the expected OIDC identity-source configuration.\n55\t- Given a MaaS deployment with no external OIDC identity source configured, when the generated AuthPolicy is inspected and authentication is exercised, then the deployment retains the existing identity behavior (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift API server's identity mapping) and RHOAI MaaS GA authentication is unchanged by this work (xKS changes are additive or configuration-driven per the strategy's backwards-compatibility NFR), measured by diffing the rendered AuthPolicy for a non-OIDC deployment against the pre-change baseline and passing the existing RHOAI MaaS GA authentication tests.\n56\t- Given the switch to the OIDC identity source, when the API key callback configuration is inspected, then the `sk-oai-*` validation path (Authorino HTTP callback to the maas-api key validation endpoint) is unchanged and OIDC-agnostic, measured by configuration inspection.\n57\t- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured to match that format, when a user with group membership authenticates, then the MaaSSubscription controller correctly maps the user to the appropriate subscription tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n58\t- Given a deployed MaaS instance, when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable (network partition or IdP downtime), then inference requests using valid API keys continue to succeed while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error message, measured by response status and error body.\n59\t- Given a MaaS deployment, when the PostgreSQL database becomes unavailable, then API key creation requests fail with HTTP 503 and OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed, measured by response status and error logs.\n60\t- The OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages is determined and documented (whether Authorino caches JWKS/validation results and for how long OIDC-authenticated requests can be served during IdP downtime), measured by a documented decision reflected in the AuthPolicy/OIDC configuration.\n61\t\n62\t## HLR Traceability\n63\t\n64\t- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity\n65\t- [P0] API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS\n66\t- [P0] Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping (supports Entra ID `groups`, Cognito `cognito:groups`, custom claims)\n67\t- [P1] Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8e8b61976ffe5b4","agent_id":"aae76ed08c444b426","session_id":"382ccfe0-6ddd-4feb-9959-cd45ee4b4e7a","uuid":"0f0dd7d8-60d5-4714-9f4d-9f90815be448","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:55:19.532Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b20549ac313a6d43","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Post-revise review RHAISTRAT-8003"}
